  "explanation": [
    "PostgreSQL has statistics for status, but not for lower(status), so it guessed: an equality matches 0.5% of the rows, 5,000 of a million. Sorting so few looked cheap, so it read every order, 1,000,000, and sorted the shipped ones by date.",
    "But shipped, Shipped and SHIPPED are 95% of the orders. The sort had 950,000 rows, 950,000 × 20 = 19,000,000, and the plan costs 20,000,000.",
    "Walking orders_created_at_idx gives the orders already sorted. It jumps to every one of them, 4 × 1,000,000 = 4,000,000, and needs no sort, so the plan costs 4,000,000. PostgreSQL has no statistics for an expression, so it guesses that an equality on one matches 0.5% of the rows, however many do. An index on lower(status) gives it statistics for the expression at the next ANALYZE."
